    <div class="container">

        <!-- Header -->
        <header class="page-header">
            <div class="subtitle">Kinematics & Motion</div>
            <h1>Maximum Height of a Projectile</h1>
        </header>

        <!-- Card: Introduction -->
        <section class="card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">📖</div>
                <h2>Introduction</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>
                    When an object is thrown into the air, it follows a curved path known as a parabola. The highest vertical point reached by the projectile during its flight is called the <strong>maximum height</strong> (denoted as $H$).
                </p>
                <p>
                    At this specific point, the vertical component of the projectile's velocity becomes zero momentarily before gravity pulls it back down. To derive the expression, we focus solely on the vertical motion of the object.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Card: Derivation -->
        <section class="card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">🧮</div>
                <h2>Step-by-Step Derivation</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ol class="step-list">
                    <li>
                        <strong>Identify Vertical Motion Parameters:</strong>
                        Consider a projectile launched with initial velocity $u$ at an angle $\theta$ to the horizontal.
                        The initial vertical velocity is:
                        $$u_y = u \sin \theta$$
                        The acceleration acting downwards is gravity: $a = -g$.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Condition at Maximum Height:</strong>
                        At the highest point ($H$), the vertical velocity becomes zero.
                        $$v_y = 0$$
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Apply Equation of Motion:</strong>
                        We use the kinematic equation that relates velocity, acceleration, and displacement (not time):
                        $$v^2 = u^2 + 2as$$
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Substitute Values:</strong>
                        Substitute vertical components into the equation:
                        $$0^2 = (u \sin \theta)^2 + 2(-g)H$$
                        Simplify:
                        $$0 = u^2 \sin^2 \theta - 2gH$$
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Solve for Height ($H$):</strong>
                        Rearrange the equation to isolate $H$:
                        $$2gH = u^2 \sin^2 \theta$$
                        $$H = \frac{u^2 \sin^2 \theta}{2g}$$
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Card: Formula (Special Component) -->
        <section class="card formula-card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">🚀</div>
                <h2>Main Formula</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>The derived expression for the maximum height is:</p>

                <div class="formula-display" id="main-formula">
                    $$H = \frac{u^2 \sin^2 \theta}{2g}$$
                </div>

                <div class="formula-actions">
                    <button class="btn btn-outline" onclick="explainFormula()">
                        <span style="margin-right: 8px;">💡</span> Explain Formula
                    </button>
                    <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="openLab()">
                        <span style="margin-right: 8px;">🔬</span> Open Formula Lab
                    </button>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Card: Variables Table -->
        <section class="card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">📊</div>
                <h2>Variable Definitions</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <div class="table-container">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Symbol</th>
                                <th>Description</th>
                                <th>SI Unit</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>$H$</td>
                                <td>Maximum Height reached</td>
                                <td>Meters (m)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>$u$</td>
                                <td>Initial velocity of projection</td>
                                <td>Meters per second (m/s)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>$\theta$</td>
                                <td>Angle of projection</td>
                                <td>Degrees or Radians</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>$g$</td>
                                <td>Acceleration due to gravity</td>
                                <td>Meters per second squared (m/s²)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Card: Important Notes -->
        <section class="card">
            <div class="card-header">
                <div class="card-icon">📝</div>
                <h2>Important Notes</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ul style="padding-left: 1.5rem; color: var(--text-secondary); font-family: 'Merriweather', serif;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">The maximum height depends on the <strong>square</strong> of the initial velocity. Doubling the speed quadruples the height.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">The maximum height is achieved when the angle of projection is $90^\circ$ (vertical throw), where $\sin 90^\circ = 1$.</li>
                    <li>At $45^\circ$, the range is maximum, but the height is not maximum.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

    </div>

